<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kisangani</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Stanleyville</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kisangani is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a> in the north-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, at the head of navigation below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyoma-falls">Boyoma Falls</a>. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> in 1883 as Stanley Falls station and later called Stanleyville, it was a centre of the Simba rebellion in 1964, and in 1999–2000 Ugandan and Rwandan armies fought each other for control of the city during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-congo-war">Second Congo War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kisangani.jpg" alt="Kisangani" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yannick Mbangi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vi_lle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kisangani stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a> in the eastern part of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-basin">Congo Basin</a>, in the north-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, about 80 kilometres from the equator. It lies at the junction of the Congo, Tshopo and Lindi rivers, below a series of seven cataracts spread over 100 kilometres between Kisangani and Ubundu with a total drop of 61 metres. Its position at the northernmost navigable point of the Congo River for large cargo made it a trading city, and it has been the commercial capital of the northern Congo since the late 19th century. It is the capital of Tshopo Province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a>, working for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> of the Belgians, founded what became Stanley Falls Station in 1883, the Wagenya people lived in the area and fished at Wagenia Falls. Stanley had first passed through in 1877, mistakenly believing he was descending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. Arab-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> slave traders from Zanzibar reached the falls from the east, and relations between Free State officials and the slavers were tense. In October 1884 Lieutenant Arvid Wester made a treaty with them, signed by a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tippu-tip">Tippu Tip</a>, which was short-lived. Tippu Tip himself became governor of the Stanley Falls District on 24 February 1887 and held it until June 1888. Arab-Swahili influence in the district was not decisively ended until 1893.</p><p>A decree of 6 September 1958, in force from 1 January 1959, established the city of Stanleyville. Late in the colonial era a boom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> brought prosperity, and the population grew from slightly over 15,000 to 121,765 by 1958. The city was the political base of the Mouvement National Congolais of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba">Patrice Lumumba</a>, who had worked there as a clerk at the central post office. In 1964 it was under siege by the Simbas. In the 1990s it served as the forward base of foreign forces advancing with Laurent-Désiré Kabila towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Kinshasa</a> to overthrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mobutu-sese-seko">Mobutu Sese Seko</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kisangani is the most important inland port of the country after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Kinshasa</a> and a commercial centre for the north-east. Its people come from many ethnic groups, and none dominates. French is the official language, and Lingala, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> and Pa-Zande are common local languages. Since the 1950s it has been a centre of higher education and research, with the Université de Kisangani among its institutions. The Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Kisangani and many other churches stand in the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kisangani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
